    "case_id": "306",
    "case_summary": "A 66-year-old woman with hypertension presented with 2 months of progressive right earache and headache without fever, 2 weeks of mild left-sided weakness, and 1 week of intermittent hallucinations and nonsensical speech. The patient was later found to have type 2 diabetes.\n\nThe patient had a left pronator drift with the remainder of examination results being unremarkable. There was no obvious manifestation of external ear canal infection during endoscopy of the right ear.\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': ['- Erythrocyte sedimentation rate: 42 mm/h (normal, <20 mm/h)', '- C-reactive protein level: 25 mg/L (normal, <10 mg/L)', '- Glycosylated hemoglobin A1C level: 6.8% (normal, 4.3%-6.1%)', '- Serological tests unremarkable for IgG4, HIV, syphilis, tuberculosis, Toxoplasma gondii, rubella virus, cytomegalovirus, and herpes simplex virus', '- CSF examination revealed no suggestive abnormalities', '- Autoimmune antibody testing: positive for anti-NMDAR antibodies (CSF titer 1:320, serum titer 1:100), CSF anti-AMPAR1 antibody positive at 1:1', '- CSF next-generation sequencing: positive for Candida tropicalis'], 'Imaging Studies': ['- CT scan of temporal bone showed otitis media, mastoiditis, and local bone destruction in the right ear', '- Brain MRI findings:', '  Figure 1A: Coronal FLAIR image demonstrated extensive area of hyperintensity with compressed right lateral ventricle', '  Figure 1B: Coronal contrast-enhanced MRI revealed heterogeneous enhancement in the dura and pia maters, with right thickened meninges extending to mastoid process', '  Figure 2A: Follow-up showing reduced brain lesion size after treatment', '  Figure 2B: Follow-up showing significantly alleviated pathological thickening of the dura mater and pia maters']}",
